# Where each setting comes from, and whether a deployed environment needs it.
#
# Read by local/scripts/doppler-check.sh and doppler-bootstrap.sh. It exists
# because the two tools disagreed about the answer: the checker classified
# settings from a hardcoded list and would demand a MinIO root password in
# production Doppler for a product project -- MinIO runs only in the local
# Docker stack. A checker that asks for things that must never exist is a
# checker people switch off.
#
#
# Format: NAME<space>CLASS[<space>SOURCE]
#
#   local        Never in Doppler. Describes the local Docker stack, or is set
#                per Doppler config rather than stored in it.
#   derived      Read from Terraform. SOURCE says how, and is required:
#                  out:<output>   a `terraform output` name. A map keyed by
#                                 environment is indexed automatically.
#                  const:<value>  the same literal in every environment.
#                  self:environment
#                                 the environment's own name.
#   supplied     Nobody can derive it. Prompted, per environment, and a
#                deployment is refused without it.
#   optional     May be set, never demanded. Prompted like supplied, but an
#                empty answer is an answer and `doppler-check` does not fail on
#                its absence.
#
#                For a setting the code is built to do without: the three
#                OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_* names until a collector exists, and the
#                Control Plane pair until there is a Control Plane -- which is
#                the documented bootstrap order (R-001) rather than a gap.
#
#                Without this class the only way to express "may be absent" was
#                to leave a setting undeclared, which is worse: nothing prompts
#                for it, nothing checks it, and the code silently keeps its
#                default forever.
#
# The SOURCE column exists because the first version assumed the setting name
# and the output name were the same. They are not -- ZITADEL_PROJECT_ID comes
# from an output called zitadel_project_ids -- so every derived value silently
# fell through to a prompt and the feature did nothing. A dry run could not show
# that; it only appeared when the lookup was tried against real outputs.

# -- Local stack only --------------------------------------------------------
# MinIO, LiteLLM and Grafana run in the local Docker stack and nowhere else. A
# deployed environment uses the real provider, so these must never appear in
# Doppler; ZITADEL_MASTERKEY belongs to the local ZITADEL container.
NODE_ENV local
MINIO_ROOT_USER local
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD local
GRAFANA_PASSWORD local
ZITADEL_MASTERKEY local

# -- Derived from Terraform ---------------------------------------------------
# Not from Terraform, but derived all the same: the environment's own name. It
# has no default in code on purpose -- a missing or misspelled value must stop a
# process rather than silently select an estate -- so a deployed service that
# does not receive it cannot start at all.
ENVIRONMENT derived self:environment
ZITADEL_PROJECT_ID derived out:zitadel_project_ids
ZITADEL_CLIENT_ID derived out:zitadel_client_ids
REDIS_URL derived out:redis_urls
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL derived out:app_urls
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL derived out:api_urls
CORS_ORIGINS derived out:cors_origins
ZITADEL_DOMAIN derived out:zitadel_domains
ZITADEL_REDIRECT_URI derived out:app_redirect_uris
ZITADEL_ADMIN_REDIRECT_URI derived out:admin_redirect_uris
STORAGE_ENDPOINT derived out:storage_endpoints
# The Supabase project's region, which its S3 gateway signs against.
STORAGE_REGION derived out:supabase_regions
{{#if service.ai_gateway}}AI_GATEWAY_URL derived out:ai_gateway_urls
{{/if}}

# -- Supplied by an operator --------------------------------------------------
{{#if service.ai_gateway}}# The AI gateway is a deployed LiteLLM proxy, not a local-only convenience:
# it has a Fly app per environment (its URL is AI_GATEWAY_URL below), and the
# master key authenticates the product to it. Operator-supplied per
# environment; the model provider keys it calls with are declared with the
# other provider credentials further down. Was marked `local`, which left a
# deployed gateway with no master key at all.
LITELLM_MASTER_KEY supplied
{{/if}}DATABASE_URL supplied
# The privileged credential migrations run as, and the only place it is used.
#
# DATABASE_URL above is the restricted role every service connects as -- it
# cannot create tables, so it cannot migrate. See local/scripts/create-app-role.sh
# and R-032.
#
# Classified here although no service reads it. This file answers "does a
# deployed environment need it", not "does an application read it", and leaving
# it out meant doppler-check.sh never asked for it -- so a missing value
# surfaced in the middle of a deploy, at the migrate step, instead of in the
# preflight that exists to catch exactly this. It was omitted on that reasoning
# and a reader found the gap by reading Doppler and not finding the secret.
DATABASE_ADMIN_URL supplied
ZITADEL_CLIENT_SECRET supplied
# Which machine identity may call this product's private platform API.
#
# The `sub` of the estate's `product-caller` service account -- an identifier,
# not a credential, which is why it is a setting rather than a secret and why
# nothing here has to be rotated when it is read, logged or copied.
#
# Supplied rather than derived because it names an account in the *platform's*
# ZITADEL instance, which this product's Terraform does not create and cannot
# output. Supplied rather than optional because the endpoints it guards refuse
# every call while it is unset: declaring it here means a deploy asks once,
# instead of a product shipping with a platform API that answers 503 and nobody
# finding out until the first customer fails to provision.
ZITADEL_PLATFORM_CALLER_SUB supplied
# Signs this application's own session cookie, and is not the provider's secret.
# At least 32 characters, and different in every environment: one shared key
# makes a development cookie a production cookie.
SESSION_SECRET supplied
# Empty is a valid answer and the right one until a collector exists: the
# tracing setup treats an empty endpoint as "no exporter", so spans are still
# created and trace context still propagates across the queue -- only the export
# is off.
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT optional
# Authentication for a hosted collector, as OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS expects
# it: `authorization=Basic <base64>`. Nothing in this codebase reads it -- the
# OpenTelemetry SDK picks it up from the environment on its own, which is why
# Grafana Cloud and the like need no code change. Empty when self-hosting or
# when there is no collector.
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS optional
# Which transport the endpoint speaks: grpc, or http/protobuf for a managed
# collector. Not inferable -- the local collector is an http:// URL that speaks
# gRPC -- and getting it wrong gives a connection error against a healthy
# gateway. The SDK ignores this variable when the exporter class is chosen in
# code, so it is read explicitly by setup_telemetry in core/observability.py.
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL optional
OTEL_SERVICE_NAME supplied
# What authorises this product's registration with the Control Plane.
#
# Not gated on a capability any more. It used to sit behind
# `control_plane_client`, which also generated a TypeScript package nothing
# imported -- one flag doing two unrelated jobs. The package is gone (F4); these
# two settings are not optional to a product, because registering is what makes
# it a product rather than an application.
#
# Not issued by the Control Plane either, whatever this comment used to say and
# `PROVISIONING_RUNBOOK.md` with it: nothing mints a per-product credential
# (F2b). It is a ZITADEL token for the estate-wide `registrar` service account.
#
# Named KORAS_CONTROL_PLANE_TOKEN because that is what Doppler holds. This was
# declared as CONTROL_PLANE_API_KEY, while `register-with-control-plane.sh` and
# the generator's registration client both read the prefixed name -- so the
# bootstrap prompted for one secret and every reader looked for another. An
# unset URL is a documented skip (R-001), which is why nothing ever failed:
# registration reported "no Control Plane configured" in an estate that had one.
KORAS_CONTROL_PLANE_TOKEN optional
# Where that Control Plane lives. It was listed as derived from an output called
# control_plane_urls, which no product emits and none can: the Control Plane is
# a separate estate with its own Terraform state, so this project has no
# resource to read the hostname off. Naming an output that cannot exist turned
# the setting into a silent prompt while the manifest claimed it was automatic.
KORAS_CONTROL_PLANE_URL optional
# No payment provider setting, and none is missing. The checkout is a page
# the provider hosts, minted by the Control Plane with its secret key and
# reached by a URL the product is handed; this product holds no provider
# credential, public or otherwise, and loads no provider script.
# Which ZITADEL project the Control Plane is. An identifier, not a credential:
# it is requested as an extra token audience at sign-in, so this product's web
# tier can read a customer's own plan from the platform's portal API while
# presenting that customer's token rather than a machine identity of its own.
#
# Optional, and the failure is quiet by design. Unset means the audience is not
# requested and no plan is read -- so anything a module gates on an entitlement
# stays hidden, and everything else is untouched. The alternative convention,
# where an unread plan grants everything, would make this setting the way to
# obtain a paid feature by deleting it.
KORAS_CONTROL_PLANE_PROJECT_ID optional
# The Supabase Storage bucket this product writes to. A name the project picks,
# not a resource this Terraform creates -- the storage module provisions no
# buckets -- so there is nothing to derive it from.
STORAGE_BUCKET supplied
# An S3 access key pair for that gateway, minted in the Supabase dashboard
# under Storage -> S3 access keys. The API signs upload and download URLs
# with it and nothing else ever holds it. Locally, MinIO's root pair.
STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY supplied
STORAGE_SECRET_KEY supplied
# For a customer whose storage policy names Cloudflare R2 or AWS S3 under
# the product's own account. Empty means the product cannot serve that
# policy and the Files page says so, rather than writing to Supabase.
STORAGE_R2_ACCESS_KEY optional
STORAGE_R2_SECRET_KEY optional
STORAGE_S3_ACCESS_KEY optional
STORAGE_S3_SECRET_KEY optional
{{#if service.ai_gateway}}# Model provider credentials, read by the gateway and by nothing else.
OPENAI_API_KEY supplied
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY supplied
# Optional providers. A routing policy may send an alias to Gemini or to
# OpenRouter; without the key the gateway answers that route with the
# provider's refusal and the runtime tries the next one.
GEMINI_API_KEY optional
OPENROUTER_API_KEY optional
{{/if}}

# -- Mail -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The product sends mail itself since 2026-09-14: the notice that an assistant
# action is waiting for approval. Any provider that speaks SMTP -- Resend,
# Postmark, SES, a mailbox -- goes here as its SMTP credentials. Optional, and
# an unset host is deliberately not an error: the sender records the message
# and logs it instead, so a product without a provider still runs and says
# what it would have sent. Locally the stack's mail catcher answers on
# SMTP_HOST=localhost with no credentials.
SMTP_HOST optional
SMTP_PORT optional
SMTP_SECURE optional
SMTP_FROM optional
SMTP_USERNAME optional
SMTP_PASSWORD optional

# -- Tunables ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by a service, sensible without being set, and until now impossible to set
# at all: absent from this file meant nothing prompted for them and nothing
# would accept them.
DATABASE_POOL_SIZE optional
# Whether X-Forwarded-For can be believed. Off by default because the header is
# set by anyone who wants to set it, and trusting it where no proxy rewrites it
# hands a caller a fresh rate-limit quota per request. Genuinely per
# environment: on behind a CDN, off in front of one.
TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR optional
{{#if capability.ai}}# Days an assistant conversation is kept after it was last touched before
# the worker's nightly sweep removes it, with its messages and actions. Usage
# rows are kept regardless. Ninety when unset.
AI_RETENTION_DAYS optional
# Days an assistant audit row is kept. A year when unset: long enough to
# answer a question about last quarter, short enough to stop growing forever.
AI_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS optional
# Model calls one organization may make in a minute, across its users: the
# abuse ceiling between the request limiter and the plan's monthly
# allowance. Thirty when unset; zero switches it off.
AI_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE optional
{{/if}}{{#if capability.reporting}}# Days a row of the general audit table is kept before the worker's nightly
# sweep removes it. A year when unset, for the same reason as the assistant's.
AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS optional
# Days a background report export is kept in the tenant's bucket before the
# exports list retires it. A week when unset.
REPORT_EXPORT_RETENTION_DAYS optional
{{/if}}